    <!-- Solutions (per-probe "how to fix", assigned manually) -->
    <string name="sol_title">How to fix</string>
    <string name="sol_boot">This is what hardware attestation (TEE) reports about the boot state. A keybox bypass (TrickyStore, TEE Simulator, OhMyKeyMint) only rewrites attestation for the apps you tell it to cover. Add this app\'s package to that tool\'s target list (e.g. target.txt) so its attestation reports a locked device with a green verified-boot state.</string>
    <string name="sol_root_detector">These installed apps look for root, emulators or a failing Play Integrity. Add the packages found here to your hiding framework (HMA-OSS) as targets, so the root apps and modules are cloaked from these detectors.</string>
    <string name="sol_spoof">This setting reads differently across the paths an app can use to read it (the spoof does not cover them all). Add the setting to the HMA-OSS spoof presets so every read path returns the same value.</string>
    <string name="sol_attest_patch">Key attestation reports a security patch level different from the system property. Set it in your keybox tool - TrickyStore in security_patch.txt (a date, or system=/boot=/vendor=/all=, or \'prop\'); OhMyKeymint in config.toml under [trust]. Both change only the attestation, not the properties.</string>
    <string name="sol_patch_prop">Build.VERSION.SECURITY_PATCH (framework) and the ro.build.version.security_patch property show different dates. Align them with resetprop. security_patch.txt and config.toml do not help here - they change only key attestation, not the properties.</string>
    <string name="note_gsf">Absent on Android 16 and later: the Google services store moved into Play Services, whose own door Google named &quot;do.not.use&quot; and which does not answer other apps. Both are tried above.</string>
